counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements can be provided that yield different results without violating the PRIMARY KEY constraint. 
-- As an example, if constraints were ignored, INSERTs like: 
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 10, 5);
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 20, 6); 
-- would show Q1 returning both rows and Q2 returning one, but this violates (c4, c2) uniqueness.
-- Under the schema, a valid dataset with unique (c4, c2) always results in identical outputs for Q1 and Q2.

